<body>

<div class="report-header">
  <h1>Global Crude Oil Price Trends</h1>
  <div class="subtitle">A Historical Analysis — 1970 to 2026</div>
  <div class="meta">Monthly Time Series &bull; {len(df)} Observations &bull; {first_date.strftime("%B %Y")} – {last_date.strftime("%B %Y")} &bull; Generated {datetime.now().strftime("%B %d, %Y")}</div>
</div>

<div class="container">

<!-- EXECUTIVE OVERVIEW -->
<div class="section">
  <h2>Executive Overview</h2>
  <p>This report analyzes {len(df)} monthly crude oil price observations from <strong>{first_date.strftime("%B %Y")}</strong> to <strong>{last_date.strftime("%B %Y")}</strong>, spanning over five decades of global energy pricing history. Prices range from a minimum of <strong>${price_min:.2f}</strong> ({min_date.strftime("%b %Y")}) to an all-time high of <strong>${price_max:.2f}</strong> ({max_date.strftime("%b %Y")}), representing a total range of <strong>${price_range:.2f}</strong> per barrel.</p>
  <p>The compound annual growth rate (CAGR) over the full period is <strong>{cagr*100:.2f}%</strong>, reflecting oil's long-term trajectory from a sub-$2 commodity to a volatile, geopolitically sensitive asset class. The <strong>{highest_avg_decade}</strong> recorded the highest average price at <strong>${highest_avg_value:.2f}/bbl</strong>.</p>
  <p>Key structural shifts include the OPEC embargo (1973), the Iranian Revolution (1979), the 1986 price collapse, the 2008 supercycle peak and crash, the 2014 shale-driven decline, and the COVID-19 demand shock (2020). The overall standard deviation of <strong>${overall_std:.2f}</strong> underscores the inherently volatile nature of crude oil pricing.</p>

  <div class="kpi-grid">
    {build_kpi_card('Overall Price Range', f'${price_range:.2f}', f'Min ${price_min:.2f} — Max ${price_max:.2f}')}
    {build_kpi_card('CAGR (1970–2026)', f'{cagr*100:.2f}%', f'${first_price:.2f} → ${last_price:.2f}')}
    {build_kpi_card('Max MoM Change', f'${abs(max_mom_change):.2f}', f'{max_mom_pct:+.1f}% in {max_mom_date.strftime("%b %Y")}')}
    {build_kpi_card('Highest Avg Decade', f'{highest_avg_decade}', f'${highest_avg_value:.2f}/bbl mean')}
    {build_kpi_card('Price Volatility (σ)', f'${overall_std:.2f}', f'Mean: ${overall_mean:.2f} | Median: ${overall_median:.2f}')}
  </div>
</div>

<!-- LONG-TERM TREND -->
<div class="section">
  <h2>Long-Term Price Trajectory (1970–2026)</h2>
  <p>The annotated chart below captures the full monthly time series. The trajectory reveals three distinct pricing eras: the <em>pre-1973 low-price regime</em> ($1–$4), the <em>volatile middle period</em> (1974–2003) with wide swings between $10 and $40, and the <em>modern supercycle era</em> (2004–present) where prices structurally shifted above $40 and frequently exceeded $100.</p>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <img src="data:image/png;base64,{chart1_b64}" alt="Full time series of crude oil prices 1970-2026">
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Key Observation:</strong> The most dramatic single-era price movement occurred during the 2008 financial crisis, when crude oil fell from its all-time high of ${price_max:.2f} (Jul 2008) to ${df[df["Date"]==pd.Timestamp("2008-12-01")]["Crude_Oil_Price"].values[0]:.2f} (Dec 2008) — a decline of {((df[df["Date"]==pd.Timestamp("2008-12-01")]["Crude_Oil_Price"].values[0] - price_max)/price_max*100):.1f}% in just five months.
  </div>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <img src="data:image/png;base64,{chart3_b64}" alt="Annual average crude oil prices 1970-2026">
  </div>
</div>

<!-- DECADE-BY-DECADE -->
<div class="section">
  <h2>Decade-by-Decade Breakdown</h2>
  <p>Grouping by decade reveals how the pricing environment has fundamentally shifted over time. Average prices, volatility, and defining events vary dramatically across eras.</p>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <img src="data:image/png;base64,{chart2_b64}" alt="Average price by decade with standard deviation">
  </div>

  <h3>Decade Summary Statistics</h3>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Decade</th><th>Mean</th><th>Median</th><th>Std Dev</th><th>Min</th><th>Max</th><th>Observations</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {build_decade_rows()}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <img src="data:image/png;base64,{chart7_b64}" alt="Box plots of price distribution by decade">
  </div>

  <h3>Decade Narratives</h3>
  {build_decade_sections()}
</div>

<!-- VOLATILITY AND SHOCKS -->
<div class="section">
  <h2>Price Volatility &amp; Major Shocks</h2>
  <p>Crude oil is among the most volatile major commodities. The chart below shows the rolling 12-month standard deviation, highlighting the clustered nature of volatility — particularly during the 1979–80, 2008–09, and 2020–22 periods.</p>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <img src="data:image/png;base64,{chart4_b64}" alt="Rolling 12-month volatility">
  </div>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <img src="data:image/png;base64,{chart5_b64}" alt="Month-over-month percentage changes">
  </div>

  <h3>Top 10 Largest Month-over-Month Price Shocks</h3>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Date</th><th>Price (USD)</th><th>MoM Change</th><th>MoM % Change</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {build_shock_rows()}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Top 10 Largest Year-over-Year Average Price Swings</h3>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Year</th><th>Avg Price</th><th>YoY Change</th><th>YoY % Change</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {build_yoy_rows()}
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

<!-- STATISTICAL SUMMARY -->
<div class="section">
  <h2>Statistical Summary &amp; Distribution</h2>
  <p>The following descriptive statistics are computed across all {len(df)} monthly observations. The distribution is right-skewed, reflecting the asymmetric nature of oil price shocks — prices can spike rapidly but tend to decline more gradually.</p>

  <div class="kpi-grid">
    {build_kpi_card('Mean', f'${overall_mean:.2f}', 'Arithmetic average')}
    {build_kpi_card('Median', f'${overall_median:.2f}', '50th percentile')}
    {build_kpi_card('Std Deviation', f'${overall_std:.2f}', 'Price dispersion')}
    {build_kpi_card('Observations', f'{len(df)}', f'{first_date.strftime("%b %Y")} – {last_date.strftime("%b %Y")}')}
  </div>

  <h3>Percentile Distribution</h3>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Percentile</th><th>5th</th><th>10th</th><th>25th</th><th>50th</th><th>75th</th><th>90th</th><th>95th</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Price (USD)</strong></td>
        <td>${percentiles[0.05]:.2f}</td>
        <td>${percentiles[0.10]:.2f}</td>
        <td>${percentiles[0.25]:.2f}</td>
        <td>${percentiles[0.50]:.2f}</td>
        <td>${percentiles[0.75]:.2f}</td>
        <td>${percentiles[0.90]:.2f}</td>
        <td>${percentiles[0.95]:.2f}</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <img src="data:image/png;base64,{chart6_b64}" alt="Distribution histogram of crude oil prices">
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Distribution Insight:</strong> The median (${overall_median:.2f}) is {'below' if overall_median < overall_mean else 'above'} the mean (${overall_mean:.2f}), confirming positive skewness — the upper tail of extreme prices (2008, 2011–2014, 2022) pulls the mean higher than the central tendency.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- OUTLOOK AND CONCLUSIONS -->
<div class="section">
  <h2>Outlook &amp; Conclusions</h2>

  <h3>Recent Trends (2020s)</h3>
  <p>The 2020s have been marked by extraordinary volatility. The COVID-19 demand shock drove prices below $21 in April 2020 — the lowest level since 2002. The subsequent recovery was amplified by supply-side constraints and geopolitical disruption (Russia-Ukraine conflict), pushing prices back above $110 in 2022. Through 2024–2026, data shows a gradual moderation toward the $60–$88 range, suggesting a normalization phase after the post-pandemic whipsaw.</p>

  <h3>Key Takeaways</h3>
  <ul style="margin: 12px 0 12px 22px; color: #3a4a5c;">
    <li><strong>Structural repricing events are rare but defining.</strong> The 1973 embargo, 2008 supercycle, and COVID-19 shock each fundamentally reset the price regime. Analysts should weight these tail events heavily in risk models.</li>
    <li><strong>Volatility clusters temporally.</strong> The rolling standard deviation analysis shows that high-volatility regimes persist for 12–24 months, not isolated months. Position sizing and hedging strategies should account for regime duration.</li>
    <li><strong>Decade averages mask intra-decade dynamics.</strong> The 2000s average of ~${decade_stats.loc["2000s", "Mean"]:.0f}/bbl obscures a range from $25 to $133. Decade-level summaries are useful for structural comparison but insufficient for tactical analysis.</li>
    <li><strong>The long-term CAGR of {cagr*100:.2f}% is deceptive.</strong> It smooths over the extreme path-dependence of oil prices. An investor who bought in 1970 and held through 2026 would have experienced multiple 50%+ drawdowns along the way.</li>
    <li><strong>Recent moderation bears watching.</strong> The decline from 2022 highs toward the mid-$60s in 2025–2026 may reflect structural demand shifts (energy transition, EV adoption) or cyclical factors (global slowdown). The data alone cannot distinguish between these drivers.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Data Limitations</h3>
  <p>All prices are in nominal USD — no inflation adjustment has been applied. Data for the most recent months (late 2025 to early 2026) may include forecasts or projections rather than realized prices. The dataset contains a single price series and does not differentiate between crude benchmarks (WTI, Brent, Dubai).</p>
</div>

</div>

<div class="footer">
  Global Crude Oil Price Trends: A Historical Analysis (1970–2026) &bull; Data: Kaggle (minahilfatima12328/global-fuel-price-trends-19702026) &bull; {len(df)} monthly observations &bull; All statistics computed from source data
</div>

</body>
</html>'''

with open('/app/output/final_report.html', 'w', encoding='utf-8') as f:
    f.write(html)

print(f"Report generated: /app/output/final_report.html ({len(html):,} bytes)")
